  <section class="section panel" data-reveal>
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The raffle lifecycle</p>
        <h2>Plain-English steps beat hidden operator magic.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>PAPR should explain the path from listing to result in public language, so trust is built through visibility instead of vague assurances.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-grid">
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <span>1. Publish the room</span>
        <h3>Show the prize, inventory, and rules.</h3>
        <p>The public page should make price, ticket count, draw mode, and eligibility easy to understand before any purchase begins.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <span>2. Protect inventory</span>
        <h3>Use short holds so the count stays real.</h3>
        <p>Cart reservations prevent oversell and automatically release inventory if payment fails or a hold expires.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <span>3. Lock and draw</span>
        <h3>Sellout closes the room, then timing takes over.</h3>
        <p>Once sold out, PAPR stops further sales and either draws immediately or assigns the raffle to the next public draw event.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
